#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590} :activity/id #uuid "de69022d-51f9-5731-9e6c-752a541e19de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135180 :tutorial/content "To **square** a number means to multiply that number by itself.\n\nFor example, to \"square $3$\", we need to multiply $3$ by $3$. We write the \"square of $3$\" as $3^2$, so we have\n\n$$\n3^2 = 3 \\cdot 3 = {\\color{blue}9}\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the square of $3$ is $9$\". We could also say \"$3$ squared equals $9$\".\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression $3^{\\color{blue}2}$ is called the **power** or **exponent**.\n\nThe act of squaring a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $3$ with the following collection of three items:\n\n![](images/4a/4a4dbb46401052829138bddc1653ae843babf4c5cfb6ba9fc40ca29ede99bd70.png)\n\nThen we can picture $3^2$ by creating a square with length and height both equal to $3$:\n\n![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png)\n\nThere are $9$ items in total in our square, indicating that $3^2 = 9$.\n\nThe first few basic squares are shown below:\n\n| $1^2=1 \\cdot 1={\\color{blue}1}$ | $2^2=2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}4}$ | $3^2=3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}9}$ | $4^2=4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}16}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| ![](images/ce/ce5db07809eb56891f31ac7fb0257edb42bd650f00c51c25857b02d1333165ad.png) | ![](images/61/6113e94a28fc50c7f1fe77ddf082d28d543aa9e2b6e65f82e8623a89f624f7db.png) | ![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png) | ![](images/7d/7d56b21746fa8a0053fa32b6c2bc6d9393e13459cb65473e64589cc2f6a01ad3.png) |\n\nThe squares of our usual counting numbers are called **square numbers** or **perfect squares**. We can write them as follows:\n$1^2, 2^2, 3^2, 4^2, 5^2, 6^2, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}4}, {\\color{blue}9}, {\\color{blue}16}, {\\color{blue}25}, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31cd70b9-a231-5c24-b6f4-1a3e714912b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8102 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "532d5ecd-c046-546b-906b-aa64daf1f6f9" :step/math-academy-id 151107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :question/id #uuid "62c086b3-028f-528b-acd5-7bdc204dcb23" :question/math-academy-id "e-12960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6f6a3fd-67df-5671-aa5d-08471b2ce1fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134405}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Positive Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "0708b802-2bb7-5823-be5b-b14cb353d3fe" :step/math-academy-id 151108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :question/id #uuid "4a393145-3e63-532b-a288-775fbc7acd15" :question/math-academy-id "e-12961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eece577d-72b8-5a0d-89fd-1cfa63bf73fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134509}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "70765661-2016-5e64-9ab9-d709f8ecd73b" :step/math-academy-id 151111 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135181 :tutorial/content "The squares of negative numbers are always positive because a negative times a negative is positive:\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(-) = (+)\n$$\n\nSome squares of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1)^{2} = (-1) \\cdot (-1) &= 1 \\\\\n(-2)^{2} = (-2) \\cdot (-2) &= 4 \\\\\n(-3)^{2} = (-3) \\cdot (-3) &= 9 \\\\\n(-4)^{2} = (-4) \\cdot (-4) &= 16 \\\\\n(-5)^{2} = (-5) \\cdot (-5) &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When squaring negative numbers, the parentheses are important!\n\nConsider the following squared quantity:\n$-3^2$\nThis is *not* the same as $(-3)^2$. Instead, this is a shorthand way of writing\n$(-1) \\cdot 3^2$.\n\nAccording to the order of operations, we should square $3$ *first* and *then* multiply by $-1$. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3^{2} &= (-1) \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&=-9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "38064eee-d6b6-5557-881a-ed03baadeed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8103 :tutorial/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "4aa26ed8-8e70-561f-a525-88bd5896de45" :step/math-academy-id 151114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :question/id #uuid "55e8797b-62bd-5515-81a5-e1c13249c1cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e8fb12-e59a-5aba-a9e8-2f872a89b915" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050287}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134406}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fa3f90d0-2a7b-5652-89e0-b78d798e323f" :step/math-academy-id 151115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :question/id #uuid "c07254e9-d6a6-55b1-bab7-4fe453c8cc69" :question/math-academy-id "e-12964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134322}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"} :step/id #uuid "bf5278f5-4ec6-5286-ae5f-3cbb68840333" :step/math-academy-id 151118}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134034}]}
